WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.670
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:09.820
This paper is about designing and creating
furniture with pieces that are geometrically

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:10.950
interlocked.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:17.029
Such pieces not only have artistic value,
but such structures can also enhance the integrity

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:19.060
and sturdiness of a piece of furniture.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:24.689
This piece of work takes a simple 2D drawing
of this interlocking structure and assembles

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:28.390
the required pieces for us to build a 3D model
from them.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.540
This drawing can be done with one of the most
user friendly modeler program out there, Google

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.540
SketchUp.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.510
This can be used even by novices.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:41.710
From these disassembled parts, it is highly
non-trivial to create a 3D printable model.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:46.740
For instance, it is required that these pieces
can be put together with one translational

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.740
motion.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.070
Basically, all we need is one nudge to put
two of these pieces together.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:56.780
If you ever had a new really simple piece
of furniture from Ikea, had a look at the

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.980
final product at the shop, and thought, "well,
I only have 10 minutes to put this thing together,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.340
but anyway, how hard can it be"?

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:09.420
And you know, 3 hours of cursing later, the
damn thing is still not completely assembled.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.520
If you had any of those experiences before,
this one push assembly condition is for you.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.789
And the algorithm automatically finds a sequence
of motions that assembles our target 3D shape,

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:26.139
and because we only have 2D information from
the input, it also has to decide how and where

00:01:26.139 --> 00:01:30.000
to extrude, thicken, or subtract from these
volumes.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.979
The search space of possible motions is immense,
and we have to take into consideration that

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:39.259
we don't even know if there is a possible
solution for this puzzle at all!

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:44.179
If this the case, the algorithm finds out
and proposes changes to the model that make

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:45.899
the construction feasible.

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:50.679
And if this wasn't enough, we can also put
this digital furniture model into a virtual

00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:56.700
world where gravitational forces are simulated
to see how stable the final result is.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.179
Here, the proposed yellow regions indicate
that the stability of this table could be

00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:03.259
improved via small modifications.

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:09.280
It is remarkable to see that a novice user
who has never done a minute of 3D modeling

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.940
can create such a beautiful and resilient
piece of furniture.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.620
Really, really nice work.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:15.620
Loving it.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:20.160
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

